   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Connectivity of Tuvalu&amp;apos;s outer islands and intra-regional locations improved</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 1</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">All Tuvalu&apos;s outer islands and destinations in the region (e.g., Fiji and Kiribati) can be reached by two safe, certified ships servicing passenger and cargo needs 
Progress/Status: Contracts for shipbuilding (Exeno Yamamizu Corporation, $23.28 million) and shipbuilding supervision (Braeamar, $749,340) were signed on 7 December 2022

Contract for the rehabilitation of port auxiliary infrastructure (fenders) (Hall Contracting Pty Ltd, AUD1,088,450) was signed on 6 March 2023. 

The Fender Refurbishment output has been completed, with the last fender installed in December 2024. Repair work of damaged fenders due to improper mooring was completed in November 2024. However, damage was again reported in February 2025. The supervision consultant has conducted a briefing session to the government in March 2025 to emphasize importance of proper mooring practice.

The shipbuilding component has been completed. The ship arrived in Funafuti, Tuvalu on 2 April 2025. The Planned maintenance system procured under the project is functioning. 
Data Source: Annual report of the MTET&apos;s Marine and Port Department 
Actual value:</narrative>
    </description>
